Czech law does not prohibit hitchhiking (''stopování''), and on ordinary roads it is legal. [https://e-sbirka.gov.cz/sb/2000/361 § 35(1) of Act No. 361/2000 Sb.] reserves the ''dálnice'' for motor vehicles built for at least 80 km/h and forbids every other road user to enter it, walk on it or ride along it — but it qualifies that ban in so many words: it applies ''mimo obslužná zařízení dálnice'', outside the motorway's service facilities, so being on foot at a motorway service area is explicitly lawful. § 36(1)(a) lets drivers stop or park only at places marked as parking, which makes those same service areas the only spot on the motorway where someone can legally pick you up. So ask people directly at a ''čerpací stanice'' or ''odpočívka'', and thumb on the on-ramp before the green motorway sign. On a ''silnice I. třídy'' and everything below it, hitchhiking is unregulated.
